# maintain_suite

The 10-fixture v1 acceptance bench. Strict gates dispatch through a
single pipeline; new fixtures must declare their per-requirement spec.

## Must
- `vacuous_test` gate fires on `implement` + `tests_pass` only, and only
  for a test file the command demonstrably RUNS (2026-09): each copied test
  is swapped for an unparseable body in the base worktree and the command
  re-run; still rc 0 means the command never loads it → reported as
  `unwired` in the outcome detail, no penalty
- A run luxe labels `step_limit_incomplete` (stopped at max steps with a
  change on disk; luxe/steplimit.py) is NOT gated and its score is
  unchanged — a complete change can also run out of steps. The bench prints
  a ⚠ on the row and `diagnostics.json` `events_kinds` carries the count;
  hand-verify before crediting (tuning_2026_10: one such PASS was broken)
- `gates_triggered` entries use ONE key, `name` (vacuous_test/orphan_file
  wrote `gate` until 2026-09 — readers of old result.json accept both)
- Diffs that feed grading run with `-M` and include the OLD side of every
  rename, so a pure rename contributes no added lines
- `regex_absent` scans the changed files PLUS every file that matched at
  base_sha; a matching base file deleted instead of edited fires the
  `deleted_target` gate
- `min_added_lines` is a floor, not a ceiling
- New fixtures land with a `requirements:` block (SpecDD Lever 1 schema)
- Bench runs use `--work-dir ~/.luxe/bench-workspace` (variance leak fix)
- Sidecar regrade via `scripts/regrade_local.py` re-runs the citation linter against `synthesizer.md`
- A run records the commit it was GRADED at (2026-10-22): run.py writes the
  workspace HEAD after `luxe maintain` to state.json `head_sha_used` and a
  `diff.patch` (base_sha_used..head_sha_used, `--binary`, best-effort) beside
  result.json. `regrade_local.py` checks out that sha, else rebuilds it from
  diff.patch on base_sha, else reports UNREGRADABLE — never the branch.
  Bench branch names are REUSED once pruned, so `origin/<branch>` can hold a
  later run's commit (one hand-verify misattributed 14–30 of 310 diffs). Runs
  without `head_sha_used` fall back to the branch, flagged ATTRIBUTION
  UNVERIFIED in the row and the totals
- Bench runs PRUNE their own branch backlog: `_prune_old_branches` keeps the
  newest `LUXE_BENCH_BRANCH_RETENTION` (25) `<prefix>/*` branches PER GOAL SLUG
  in both the workspace clone and the fixture-cache origin, and logs what it
  dropped. Grouping is per slug because `luxe.pr.plan_branch_name`'s 99-name
  budget is per slug — a repo-wide cap would still let one hot slug die.
  Retention, not delete-after-grading: `regrade_local.py` falls back to the
  pushed branch for runs without `head_sha_used`, and a pruned branch's
  commit is gc-able, so recent ones must survive. `keep<=0` is a no-op, never a wipe
- Borderline document/manage fixtures replicate 3× before claiming a regression
- `destructive_diff` catches REPLACE-IN-PLACE, not just lopsided churn
  (2026-08-19): its ratio prong needs deletions >= 5x additions, so a
  same-sized overwrite has ratio 1.0 and walked through — two 2026-08-17
  bake-off diffs passed at 4/5 with an empty gate list. Prong 2 fires on a
  per-file replace band (>= 30 deleted lines, additions 0.5-2x deletions) when
  BOTH loss readings agree that information left: >= 80% of distinct content
  words AND >= 60% of word occurrences absent from the whole diff's added
  lines. Both readings are required because each alone has a shape that fools
  it — occurrences are propped up by shared keywords, distinct words are
  retired wholesale by a mechanical rename. Added tokens pool across every
  file so extract-a-module refactors stay clean; generated/lock files are
  skipped. Prong 3 (document/manage, markdown) fires on a section heading
  deleted with >= 3 body lines, not retitled among the added headings, in a
  file that did not grow past 1.2x what it lost. Thresholds are calibrated
  against 400 commits of real history — prongs 2 and 3's `_check_*` helpers
  carry the numbers; DON'T loosen them without re-running that audit
- Prong 3 reads headings OUTSIDE fenced code only (2026-10-21): a `# comment`
  in a ```bash block is body text, not a section. Fence state comes from the
  whole file at each side of the diff (`_doc_headings`), never from hunk lines
  — a hunk can open mid-fence. A side git can't read keeps the old reading, so
  the filter can only remove firings. Audit: `acceptance/grader_fixes_2026_10/`
- `min_added_lines` counts a MARKDOWN line as ceil(len/80) lines
  (2026-10-21): the floor measures prose added, not wrapping. Code lines
  always count 1. The count can only rise, so no earlier pass flips; don't
  replace it with per-fixture word floors tuned on observed outputs
- `tool_output_echo` is its OWN gate, never folded into `destructive_diff`:
  >= 10 consecutively numbered `<n>\t` added lines is `read_file`'s rendering
  (src/luxe/tools/fs.py) written back into a file, and the remediation differs.
  The information-loss prong structurally cannot see it — a line-numbered copy
  of a file preserves every word
- A git failure during GRADING is a harness ERROR, never a verdict
  (2026-08-12): the grader's diff/grep helpers (grade.py, gates.py) raise
  `GitRunError` (proc.py's `run_git`) instead of
  returning the empty-diff shape ([] / (0,0) / ""), and run.py's per-fixture
  handler records the fixture as ERRORED — the scoreboard already separates
  that from FAILED. Before this, an index.lock race or corrupt base_sha
  graded every diff-dependent requirement as "the model changed nothing".
  `git grep` keeps rc 1 as a legitimate no-match (ok_codes=(0,1)); the
  vacuous-test worktree gate keeps its documented fail-open (it only ever
  removes points)
- The strict gates (`check_*`, `apply_strict_gates`, their thresholds) live
  in `gates.py` (2026-10-22); `grade_fixture` is their caller and the git
  readers that build their inputs (`_diff_hunks`, `_doc_headings`) stay in
  grade.py. Both share `proc.py`'s subprocess layer — gates.py never imports
  grade.py
- ONE fixtures.yaml loader (2026-09): `fixtures.py:load_fixtures` host-remaps
  the `/Users/<someone>/.luxe/fixture-cache/...` paths the YAML pins; run.py,
  `scripts/regrade_local.py` and `scripts/_archive/opencode_harness.py` all read
  through it. run.py preflights every local origin (`origin_problem`: exists,
  objects/ + refs/heads/ writable) and refuses to start otherwise
- An ENVIRONMENT failure is an ERROR, never a score: a failed `push` step in
  pr_state.json errors the fixture (the grade stays in result.json for
  inspection), and regrade_local reports a branch it cannot find as
  UNREGRADABLE (excluded from totals) instead of grading base_sha
- A diff luxe never SHIPPED is an ERROR, never a score. If pr_state.json's
  commit step failed with failed_no_mutations_produced while base_sha..HEAD
  differs, the work was committed outside luxe's view. Luxe's commit step now
  ships agent-made commits that descend from base_sha, so this guard catches
  only a disagreement, not a normal run (2026-09: before that, every
  deps-audit run scored 4/5 on an unpushed commit)
- A fixture's reported outcome comes from its STATE: a fresh attempt deletes
  the previous result.json/diagnostics.json first; a SKIP_DONE replay of an
  ERROR state reports ERROR (never the stale file), and replays are flagged
  `Diagnostics.cached`
- Reusing a clone DETACHES at base_sha (`checkout -f --detach`) — never
  `reset --hard` on the checked-out branch, which rewound the previous run's
  `luxe/...` branch to base
- The per-fixture timeout kills luxe's whole process group
- A model bake-off clears `preflight.py` on the bench host before the run
  (2026-10): static (weights/shards, arch resolvable by the installed oMLX,
  engine, quant parity vs the FIRST arm, tool template) + `--live`
  structured tool call. The live tool call is the authoritative parser
  gate — oMLX picks parsers through several layers, so preflight never
  guesses one statically. A FAIL drops that arm, never the bake-off

## Must not
- Run bench without `--keep-loaded` (post-run unload fires by default)
- Let the branch backlog grow unpruned. Before 2026-08-11 nothing removed bench
  branches, so a fixture died permanently at ~99 runs on one goal with
  `PRError: Cannot find a free branch name`. Because A/B arms run sequentially
  the arm running SECOND absorbed the exhaustion and its fixtures scored 0,
  which the scoreboard renders as an ordinary failure — it manufactured a
  clean-looking 116-vs-108 "regression" that was pure arm ordering. See
  `acceptance/pwir_rerun_2026_08_10/BRANCH-LEAK.md`
- Trust a `scripts/regrade_local.py` row flagged ATTRIBUTION UNVERIFIED: it
  read `origin/<branch>`, which a later run may have reused. Hand-verify it by
  resolving that run's commit from the reflog, never `base..<branch name>`
- Add fixtures without `task_type` in the YAML (cve_lookup gating depends on it)

## Owns
- benchmarks/maintain_suite/**
